From: Dennis Gilmore <dennis@ausil.us>
Subject: [PATCH] draw frames from the wall clock when the stream has no timing

p7sim: draw frames from the wall clock when the stream has no timing

readthread() only advances the display clock by the delay field (bits 23-31)
of each word it receives, and it only calls draw() from inside that clock:

	while(time > frmtime) { ... process(frmtime); signal_draw(); }

The PiDP-1 pdp1 (pidp1-mods; src/pdp1/netPorts.c serves the Type 30 display
on 3400-3403) sends every word with a zero delay, so "time" never leaves 0,
draw() never runs, and neither the phosphor nor the window is ever painted:
p7sim connects, takes thousands of points a second, and shows a black window
forever.  On Wayland it is worse, since a window that never swaps its buffers
is never mapped at all.

Fall back to the wall clock when the stream carries no timing, which paces the
display at the same ~30fps the timing path would have used.  Streams that do
carry delays are untouched.

Also say something when the emulator hangs up instead of exiting silently:
display.c dropClient() closes the socket whenever a second client takes the
screen or a client stalls, and readthread() did a bare exit(0), so the window
just vanished with no explanation.
---
--- a/main.c
+++ b/main.c
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <string.h>
 #include <stdarg.h>
+#include <errno.h>
 #include <stdint.h>
 #include <ctype.h>
 
@@ -747,11 +748,24 @@
 simtime = 0;
 realtime = realtime_start;
 
+	// Some emulators (the PiDP-1 pdp1, for one) send every word with a zero
+	// delay, so the display clock below never advances, draw() is never
+	// called, and the screen never shows anything.  Fall back to the wall
+	// clock for those streams so the phosphor still ages ~30 times a second.
+	uint64 walllast = SDL_GetPerformanceCounter();
+	uint64 walltime = 0;
+
 	time = 0;
 	int esc = 0;
 for(;;){
 	nbytes = read(netfd, cmds, sizeof(cmds));
-if(nbytes <= 0) break;
+if(nbytes <= 0) {
+	if(nbytes < 0)
+		fprintf(stderr, "%s: display read: %s\n", argv0, strerror(errno));
+	else
+		fprintf(stderr, "%s: display closed the connection\n", argv0);
+	break;
+}
 if((nbytes % 4) != 0) printf("yikes %d\n", nbytes), exit(1);
 	ncmds = nbytes/4;
 
@@ -791,6 +805,20 @@
 
 		// we hope draw is finished before we decide to flip again
 		// 30fps should be doable
+		if(time == 0) {
+			// no timing in the stream, see walllast above
+			uint64 wallnow = SDL_GetPerformanceCounter();
+			walltime += (wallnow - walllast) * 1000000ULL / SDL_GetPerformanceFrequency();
+			walllast = wallnow;
+			while(walltime > frmtime) {
+				walltime -= frmtime;
+simtime += frmtime;
+realtime = SDL_GetPerformanceCounter() - realtime_start;
+wait_canprocess();
+				process(frmtime);
+signal_draw();
+			}
+		}
 		while(time > frmtime) {
 			time -= frmtime;
 simtime += frmtime;
